        'A gauge R&R splits the random measurement spread into repeatability (one person, same part, again) and reproducibility (differences between people), as in the first lesson of this topic.\n\nThe result is given two ways: as % of tolerance, the gauge’s spread (6σ, six standard deviations) divided by the tolerance width, or as % of study variation, the gauge’s spread divided by the total spread of the parts in the study.',
        'The two answer different questions. % of tolerance: can I tell good from bad against the spec (the specification limits)? % of study variation: can I see differences between parts, which you need for SPC (statistical process control: following a process with control charts) and improvement work?\n\nThe common AIAG limits apply to both: under 10% good, 10 to 30% acceptable depending on use and cost, over 30% not acceptable. Many customers have their own rules, so check the customer manual.',
        'Study variation depends on which parts you pick. Ten parts from one hour barely differ, so even a good gauge looks poor next to them. Pick parts that cover the real process spread, across days and lots.\n\nThe ndc, the number of distinct categories, says how many groups the gauge can tell apart within that spread; AIAG asks for at least 5. With an ndc of 2, the gauge can only say “big” or “small”, which is useless on a control chart.',
        'Read the split, because the fixes differ completely. High repeatability points to the gauge or fixture (the device that holds the part): play, poor clamping, the wrong stylus (the tip that touches the part), dirt on the part.\n\nHigh reproducibility points to method: different technique, different measuring spots, an unclear instruction. What you hear on the floor: “the night shift measures differently”. Usually the instruction never said where to measure.',
        'R&R is about spread, not truth: bias, linearity and stability need their own checks against a master. And the gauge must show small enough steps. Resolution, the smallest step on the readout, should be at most a tenth of the tolerance: the rule of ten.\n\nSharp question: “Was this study done with parts from across the process spread, by the people who measure every day?” A study with hand-picked parts and the lab technician says little about the floor.',
