        'The study asks three questions. Does each inspector agree with themselves (their repeatability)? Do the inspectors agree with each other? Do they agree with the expert standard?\n\nAgreement is often expressed as kappa, a score corrected for what you would get by pure guessing: 1 is perfect, 0 is no better than chance. AIAG guidance: above 0.75 good, below 0.4 poor. Ninety percent agreement can still be a poor kappa when almost every part is good.',
        'Look at both error types. An escape is a bad part that passes: the customer’s risk. A false reject is a good part that is rejected: your own cost, in scrap and rework.\n\nInspectors drift towards one side under pressure. After a customer complaint, false rejects climb; at the end of a long shift, escapes climb. Ask for both numbers separately; one “accuracy” figure hides which way the errors go.',
        'Even good people miss defects. Studies of 100% visual inspection often report that about 80% of defects are found, with a wide range of roughly 70 to 90%, and less with fatigue, rare defects and fast lines (Sandia literature review, See, 2012).\n\nA second inspector helps but doesn’t make it perfect. That is why “we added 100% inspection” is containment: a temporary measure that protects the customer while you find the cause, not a fix.',
